{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"# Design: bound the READ, do not retain — enrichment queue + digest history\n\nDesign work for this ticket. Measurements are from this repo's live tracker at\n`/Users/joeoakhart/rebar/.tickets-tracker` (3,727 ticket dirs, 53,495 event files) on\n2026-08-09; the two-clone check is `tests/integration/test_concurrency_regression.py`\n(27 passed, 166.8 s).\n\n## 1. Where the cost actually is (measured, AC1)\n\nPer-ticket accumulation of the four event types this ticket names:\n\n| type | files | tickets | median/ticket | max/ticket | on disk |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| ENQUEUE_ENRICH | 1,885 | 554 | 3 | 32 | |\n| CLAIM_ENRICH | 1,545 | 552 | 2 | 32 | |\n| DONE_ENRICH | 1,547 | 552 | 2 | 32 | |\n| (three combined) | 4,978 | — | — | — | 2,340 KiB, mean 481 B |\n| TICKET_DIGEST | 1,548 | 552 | 2 | 32 | 3.03 MiB, mean 2,053 B |\n\nDirents per ticket dir: median 9, mean 14.4, max 254.\n\n**Storage is not the problem.** All queue + digest events together are ~5.3 MiB of a\n~251 MiB working tree (~2%). And per the earlier comment on this ticket, deleting a\ncommitted file reclaims *zero* repository space — the blob stays in git history — so a\nretention scheme could at best shave ~2% off checkout size while reclaiming nothing from\nthe 1.02 GiB pack.\n\n**Read cost is the problem, and it is asymmetric between the two readers.**\n\n- `digest_sidecar.latest_ticket_digest` is *already* bounded: it sorts dirents and walks\n  newest→oldest, returning on the first parseable payload. Normal case = 1 `listdir` +\n  **1 file open**.\n- `llm/overlap/queue.py::_events_of` opens and `json.load`s **every** matching file, and\n  `reduce_ticket` calls it three times per ticket. Full-store counts, measured:\n\n  | reduce strategy | listdir calls | file opens | wall |\n  |---|---|---|---|\n  | current `_events_of` (all files) | 11,181 | **4,977** | 0.372 s |\n  | newest-first suffix read | 11,181 | **1,658** | 0.298 s |\n\nThis is not theoretical. `enrich_drain.maybe_drain` calls `_queue.pending_enrichment` as\nits \"cheap gate\" on every write, against a 20 ms budget\n(`overlap_drain_gate_budget_ms`). Every rebar command run against this checkout today logs\n`enrich drain gate check took 404.9 ms (> 20 ms budget)` — observed repeatedly during this\nsession at 404–544 ms. The gate is ~20x over budget **now**, and the opens term grows\nwithout bound while the budget does not.\n\n## 2. The design (AC2, AC4, AC5)\n\n**Do not add retention. Bound the read.**\n\nThe queue reduction is latest-wins per event type, and the event filename is\n`{timestamp}-{uuid}-{TYPE}.json` where that prefix is the *same* monotonic timestamp the\nreducer compares (`_store/event_append.py::event_filename`, and `_prepare_event` builds the\nname from the validated event's own timestamp). Timestamps are fixed-width ns integers, so\nlexicographic filename order **is** timestamp order. Therefore:\n\n- `_latest(dir, TYPE)` needs the **last** matching dirent only: sort names, open the\n  newest, fall back to the next-older on a parse failure — the identical shape\n  `latest_ticket_digest` already uses. **1 open per type per ticket.**\n- `claim()` arbitration needs CLAIM events *newer than the latest ENQUEUE*. Those are a\n  contiguous **suffix** of the sorted names, so it can walk newest→oldest and stop as soon\n  as a filename's timestamp prefix is `<=` the enqueue timestamp. Bounded by the number of\n  claims in the current enqueue epoch (the contention window), not by history.\n\n**Documented bound.** Per ticket: `O(dirents)` for the directory listing, and file opens\n`<= 3 + k` for `reduce_ticket` (one each for the newest ENQUEUE / CLAIM / DONE, plus at\nmost `k` extra on parse-failure fallback, `k` = consecutive corrupt newest files), and\n`<= c` for `claim()` arbitration where `c` = CLAIM events since the latest ENQUEUE. For\n`latest_ticket_digest`: `O(dirents)` + `1` open (already true today). No term is a function\nof total history.\n\n**Merge-safety argument.** The scheme removes nothing, renames nothing, and writes nothing.\nEvery committed UUID event stays exactly where it was written, so two clones that\nindependently apply this change produce byte-identical trees to two clones that do not; the\nunion merge is unchanged and invariant I1 (`docs/concurrency.md` §I1, \"Never modify or delete\nan existing event file\") is untouched. Reconvergence is not merely preserved, it is\n*unaffected* — which is the strongest form of AC2/AC5 available. The existing two-clone\nregression added by be8a0c0595 in `tests/integration/test_concurrency_regression.py` remains\nthe executable statement of that property and passes (27 passed, 166.8 s); a read-only change\ncannot regress it, and any future variant that does delete would (AC3).\n\n## 3. Why every deletion-based retention scheme was rejected\n\n**The mechanism, precisely.** The 8185/55bc wedge is not \"deletion is unsafe\". It is:\n`delete(A)` + `add(B_uuid1)` in clone 1, `delete(A)` + `add(B_uuid2)` in clone 2. Git's\nrename detection pairs the shared `delete(A)` with a *different* add on each side, producing\n`CONFLICT (rename/rename)`.\n\nCompaction — the one sanctioned I1 exception — is safe precisely because it avoids that\nshape: `compact.py` does `retired = fp + RETIRED_SUFFIX; os.rename(fp, retired)`. The target\nname is **deterministic** (derived from the source, so both clones compute the same path) and\nthe content is **byte-identical**. Both sides therefore present the same rename, and the\n100%-similarity candidate wins pairing unambiguously.\n\nThat yields the rule any future retention must satisfy:\n\n> A merge-safe removal must be a rename to a **deterministic, source-derived** path with\n> **identical content**. A removal paired with a fresh-UUID add is never merge-safe.\n\nOptions considered and rejected:\n\n- **Physical prune (keep N newest)** — the 8185/55bc defect. Rejected: violates I1, breaks\n  reconvergence, and reclaims no repository space.\n- **`merge.renames = false` on the tracker to make prune merge cleanly** (the experiment in\n  the prior comment, verified 3/3). Rejected *as a license for deletion*: correctness would\n  depend on a config being present in **every** clone, including clones created outside\n  `rebar init` (a plain `git clone` of the tickets branch, a CI checkout, a manually made\n  worktree). One clone missing the key re-wedges the merge, and the failure is silent until\n  it isn't. It is still worth adopting as **defence in depth** — rename detection has no\n  legitimate role in a UUID-named append-only store — but that is a separate change and must\n  not be the load-bearing part of any retention scheme.\n- **`.retired` renames for sidecar events** (extend the compaction pattern). Merge-safe by\n  the rule above, and the only deletion-shaped option that is. Rejected for *this* subject\n  anyway: it buys ~2% of checkout size, reclaims nothing from the pack, adds a second retire\n  lifecycle to reason about, and does nothing for the read cost that the suffix-lazy reader\n  fixes for free.\n- **Append-only checkpoint event** (write a COMPACT/CHECKPOINT summarising prior events;\n  readers prefer it). Merge-safe (pure add), but it *increases* bytes and only helps read\n  cost — which the suffix-lazy read already bounds at zero cost. Strictly dominated.\n\n## 4. Limits\n\n- **The `listdir` term is not bounded by this design.** Reads stay `O(dirents per ticket\n  dir)`, and `pending_enrichment` pays that for **every** ticket dir in the store (3,727\n  today, 11,181 `listdir` calls per gate check). The measured 0.372 s → 0.298 s improvement\n  is modest precisely because listdir, not open, dominates at today's scale. The opens term\n  is the one that grows without bound, so this fixes the *asymptote*, not today's constant.\n  Getting the constant under the 20 ms budget needs a separate lever (a store-level index, or\n  gating the drain on something cheaper than a full-store scan) — out of scope here, but the\n  reason the follow-up should not claim the budget breach is solved.\n- **Correctness of the suffix read depends on filename-prefix == event-timestamp.** That\n  holds today by construction (`event_filename` is built from the validated event's own\n  timestamp) but it is an *implicit* coupling. The follow-up should pin it with a test, or the\n  optimisation silently mis-reduces if a future writer ever names files differently.\n- **Fixed-width timestamps are load-bearing.** Lexicographic order equals numeric order only\n  while ns timestamps stay 19 digits. True until the year 2286; worth a comment, not a guard.\n- **Corrupt-newest fallback changes the failure mode.** Reading all files means a corrupt file\n  is skipped and an older good one still contributes; reading newest-first means a corrupt\n  newest file costs one extra open per fallback step. Bounded and already the digest\n  sidecar's accepted behaviour, but it is a behaviour change, not a pure refactor.\n- **`.retired` GC is still unsolved and unaffected.** `docs/concurrency.md` keeps retired files\n  permanently, with a branch-wide sweep tracked as `536b-8930-b922-4063` (status `idea`). This\n  design does not touch it.\n- **This is measured on one store.** 3,727 tickets, max 32 queue events on any single ticket.\n  A store with a pathological hot ticket (thousands of re-certifications) would shift the\n  balance between the listdir and open terms; the design is still correct, but the measured\n  ratio is not universal.\n\n## 5. What lands where (AC6)\n\nThe design above is the deliverable of THIS ticket. The code change — the suffix-lazy reader\nin `src/rebar/llm/overlap/queue.py` plus the test pinning the filename/timestamp coupling — is\na behaviour-affecting production change to a hot path with its own file impact, its own\nreview, and its own CI run. It is filed as a linked follow-up rather than smuggled into a\ndesign ticket, and this ticket does not claim the read bound is *implemented* — only that it\nis designed, measured, and argued safe."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786311483251261001,"uuid":"c322d2fa-6200-4c8d-b7f0-dad658b10689"}